# Test-specific relaxations, inheriting everything else from the repo root.
#
# Each of these is disabled because the check is measuring the wrong thing in a
# Catch2 test, not because the tests are exempt from standards.
---
InheritParentConfig: true
Checks: >
  -readability-function-cognitive-complexity,
  -cert-msc32-c,
  -cert-msc51-cpp,
  -modernize-use-designated-initializers,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-do-while,
  -readability-container-size-empty,
  -bugprone-unchecked-optional-access,

# Why each:
#
#   readability-function-cognitive-complexity
#     Catch2 compiles every SECTION into one generated function, so a test case
#     with six sections is reported as a single 60-complexity function that does
#     not exist in the source. Splitting the sweeps into helpers to satisfy the
#     metric would make the tests harder to read, not easier.
#
#   cert-msc32-c / cert-msc51-cpp  ("RNG seeded with a constant")
#     Constant seeds are the entire point. A benchmark or a kernel-equivalence
#     sweep that generated different data on each run would not be reproducible,
#     and a fuzz failure could not be replayed. Randomness belongs in the fuzzer,
#     which has a real entropy source.
#
#   modernize-use-designated-initializers
#     `field{55, "AAPL"}` reads better than `field{.tag = 55, .value = "AAPL"}`
#     when it appears a hundred times in expected-value tables. Library code
#     still uses designated initializers.
#
#   cppcoreguidelines-avoid-do-while
#     Catch2's assertion macros expand to do/while(0).
#
#   readability-container-size-empty
#     Tests deliberately assert on .size() == 0 to check the exact value rather
#     than the emptiness predicate.
#
#   bugprone-unchecked-optional-access
#     The check cannot see through Catch2's REQUIRE, so
#     `REQUIRE(o.has_value()); CHECK(*o == x);` is reported as unchecked even
#     though REQUIRE aborts the test case on failure. Library code keeps the
#     check enabled, where it is genuinely useful.
